Original commit body from 8624110617b2d8306bd2f1c164f1a8cab311391f:

The co-change scan flagged 28 files on 2026-05-15 and 87 on 2026-08-18 with
no docstring having drifted in between. The growth was an artifact, and
diagnosing it wrong the first time is why this commit is shaped as it is.

ROOT CAUSE. `delta_days = ds_age - body_age` is only meaningful while the
docstring's date can still move. Three mass commits PINNED it for most of
the corpus -- the ADR-0010 monorepo reorg (blame follows the move), the
Phase-3/4 TreeSitterAnalyzer migrations (~106 analyzers stamped with
identical orchestration boilerplate), and the ADR-3bbb subcategory sweep
(one line prepended to 45 linker docstrings). 55 of the 87 flagged files had
their ds date pinned by one of nine such commits. With ds frozen and the
clock running, delta grows by itself: the ds=2026-02-19 cohort could not
fire at ANY body age on 2026-05-15 (ds_age was 85, under the 90 minimum) and
fired for anything touched within 89 days by 2026-08-18.

WHAT I TRIED FIRST AND WHY IT IS NOT HERE. The obvious fix is to detect
those sweeps and look past them to the last real edit. It cannot be done
from blame, and the measurement is unambiguous:

    3f29f139b1  ADR-3bbb convention stamp   median 1 line   2% of docstring
    c9406ccd54  staleness-audit fix         median 2 lines  4% of docstring

The first is a prefix nobody read; the second is a line-by-line review. Same
size, similar file counts -- neither ownership share nor co-floor count
separates them. Worse, five of the nine detected "sweeps" ARE prior
staleness-audit fix commits, so that rule would have made each audit blind
the next one to exactly the files it had just verified.

WHAT IS HERE INSTEAD.

1. Disclosure, not inference. Every row reports its floor commit, the share
   of the docstring that commit owns, and how many other docstrings it
   floors -- `ds@3f29f139b1[2%/+21]`. Facts; the semantic step judges. The
   header lists every commit flooring >= 8 docstrings, with its subject.

2. A metric that does not decay. `body_commits_since_ds` counts commits that
   landed on the body since anyone touched the docstring. "56 commits have
   landed on py.py since its docstring moved" means the same thing next year.

3. The day arm applies only where the floor is particular to the file.
   Pinning is the decay mechanism, so on a pinned row `delta >= 90` is a
   clock reading, not evidence.

Effect: 87 -> 47, and the ranking inverts usefully. py.py (56 commits),
cli.py (34), sketch.py (27), analyze/base.py (25) now lead; none appeared in
the old day-ranked pool, which was topped by racket.py and rst.py.

Each row now says which arm fired, so day-only rows can be discounted.

